sec.p:
On each trial, the observer is presented with two identical reference stimuli, denoted x0, and one comparison stimulus, denoted x1 = x0 + Δ where Δ represents a small offset from the reference. Our model assumes that these three stimuli are independently encoded into an internal representational space by a noisy process, which we assume to follow a multivariate Gaussian distribution. Formally,
(1)
z0∼N(x0,Σ(x0)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} z_{0} \sim \mathcal{N}\left(x_{0}, \Sigma\left(x_{0}\right)\right) \tag{1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(2)
z0′∼N(x0,Σ(x0)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} z_{0}^{\prime} \sim \mathcal{N}\left(x_{0}, \Sigma\left(x_{0}\right)\right) \tag{2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(3)
z1∼N(x0+Δ,Σ(x0+Δ)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} z_{1} \sim \mathcal{N}\left(x_{0}+\Delta, \Sigma\left(x_{0}+\Delta\right)\right) \tag{3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where z0,z0′,z1\begin{document}$z_{0}, z_{0}^{\prime}, z_{1}$\end{document} denote the internal representations derived from the two reference and the comparison stimuli, respectively. Our model posits that the observer correctly identifies z1 as representing the comparison stimulus (i.e. the “odd-one-out”) if
(4)
dM2(z0,z0′)−min(dM2(z0,z1),dM2(z0′,z1))<0, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} d_{M}^{2}\left(z_{0}, z_{0}^{\prime}\right)-\min \left(d_{M}^{2}\left(z_{0}, z_{1}\right), d_{M}^{2}\left(z_{0}^{\prime}, z_{1}\right)\right)<0, \tag{4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where dM2(⋅,⋅)\begin{document}$d_{M}^{2}(\cdot, \cdot)$\end{document} denotes the squared Mahalanobis distance for a selected pair of internal representations, formulated as
(5)
dM2(z0,z0′)=(z0−z0′)⊤S−1(z0−z0′) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} d_{M}^{2}\left(z_{0}, z_{0}^{\prime}\right)=\left(z_{0}-z_{0}^{\prime}\right)^{\top} \mathbf{S}^{-1}\left(z_{0}-z_{0}^{\prime}\right) \tag{5} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(6)
dM2(z0,z1)=(z0−z1)⊤S−1(z0−z1) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} d_{M}^{2}\left(z_{0}, z_{1}\right)=\left(z_{0}-z_{1}\right)^{\top} \mathbf{S}^{-1}\left(z_{0}-z_{1}\right) \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(7)
dM2(z0′,z1)=(z0′−z1)⊤S−1(z0′−z1), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} d_{M}^{2}\left(z_{0}^{\prime}, z_{1}\right)=\left(z_{0}^{\prime}-z_{1}\right)^{\top} \mathbf{S}^{-1}\left(z_{0}^{\prime}-z_{1}\right), \tag{7} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where S is the weighted average of the covariance across the reference and the comparison stimuli, that is,
(8)
S=23⋅Σ(x0)+13⋅Σ(x0+Δ). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathbf{S}=\frac{2}{3} \cdot \Sigma\left(x_{0}\right)+\frac{1}{3} \cdot \Sigma\left(x_{0}+\Delta\right) . \tag{8} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

The covariance matrix field is constructed using one-dimensional Chebyshev polynomial basis functions (Chebyshev, 1853). We chose Chebyshev polynomials because they allow for the expression of smoothness over a bounded interval without imposing periodic boundary conditions. Letx = [xdim1, xdim2] denote a location in the 2D model space. The basis functions are defined recursively for each model space dimension as given here for xdim1:
(9)
T0(xdim1)=1 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle T_{0}\left(x_{\operatorname{dim} 1}\right)=1$$\end{document}
(10)
T1(xdim1)=xdim1, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} T_{1}\left(x_{\mathrm{dim} 1}\right)=x_{\mathrm{dim} 1}, \tag{10} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(11)
Ti+1(xdim1)=2xdim1⋅Ti(xdim1)−Ti−1(xdim1), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} T_{i+1}\left(x_{\mathrm{dim} 1}\right)=2 x_{\mathrm{dim} 1} \cdot T_{i}\left(x_{\mathrm{dim} 1}\right)-T_{i-1}\left(x_{\mathrm{dim} 1}\right), \tag{11} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where xdim1, Ti(xdim1) ∈ ℝn, and n is the number of discretized points along that stimulus dimension, which can be chosen flexibly to achieve any desired resolution. We construct two-dimensional basis functions by taking the outer product:
(12)
ϕi,j(x)=Ti(xdim1)⋅Tj(xdim2), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \phi_{i, j}(x)=T_{i}\left(x_{\operatorname{dim} 1}\right) \cdot T_{j}\left(x_{\operatorname{dim} 2}\right), \tag{12} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where ϕi,j ∈ ℝn×m, with n = m representing the number of discretized points along each dimension of the model space. We limited the number of basis functions to five per dimension, i.e., i, j ∈ {0, 1, …, 4}, resulting in a total of 5 × 5 = 25 2D basis functions (Figure 4, first panel). The polynomial order of each 2D basis function is given by i + j, with higher-order basis functions describing more rapidly varying patterns.

The basis functions were weighted by a learnable parameter matrix, W ∈ ℝ5×5×2×3, where the first two dimensions index the Chebyshev basis functions along each model space dimension (i, j ∈ {0, 1, …, 4}), and the last two dimensions index the output components (k ∈ {1, 2} and l = {1, 2, 3}). The weighted basis functions are expanded into an overcomplete representation Uk,l ∈ ℝn×m (Figure 4, second panel) as the following,
(13)
Uk,l(x)=∑i=04∑j=04Wi,j,k,l⋅ϕi,j(x). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathbf{U}_{k, l}(x)=\sum_{i=0}^{4} \sum_{j=0}^{4} W_{i, j, k, l} \cdot \phi_{i, j}(x) . \tag{13} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

This weighted sum overcomplete representation was then combined with its own transpose to yield a positive semi-definite covariance matrix (Figure 4, third panel), Σ(x) ∈ ℝ2×2 for x at any discretized point in the model space, that is,
(14)
Σ(x)=[σdim12σdim1,dim2σdim1,dim2σdim22]=Uk,l(x)⋅Uk,l(x)T. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \Sigma(x)=\left[\begin{array}{cc} \sigma_{\operatorname{dim} 1}^{2} & \sigma_{\operatorname{dim} 1, \operatorname{dim} 2} \tag{14}\\ \sigma_{\operatorname{dim} 1, \operatorname{dim} 2} & \sigma_{\operatorname{dim} 2}^{2} \end{array}\right]=\mathbf{U}_{k, l}(x) \cdot \mathbf{U}_{k, l}(x)^{T} .$$\end{document}

We imposed a weak prior over the weight matrix W. Specifically, we assumed that each weight was distributed a priori as a zero-mean one-dimensional Gaussian,
(15)
Wi,j,k,l∼N(0,ηi+j), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} W_{i, j, k, l} \sim \mathcal{N}\left(0, \eta_{i+j}\right), \tag{15} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where η represents the variance of each weight and it decays exponentially with i+j, which denotes the polynomial order of the corresponding 2D basis function, that is,
(16)
ηi+j=γ⋅ϵi+j. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \eta_{i+j}=\gamma \cdot \epsilon^{i+j} . \tag{16} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

We computed the negative log-likelihood of any hypothesized weight matrix given the participant’s binary responses yr as follows:
(17)
pr(y1,…,yr∣W)=∑r=1R(yr⋅log⁡(pr)+(1−yr)⋅log⁡(1−pr)), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p_{r}\left(y_{1}, \ldots, y_{r} \mid \mathbf{W}\right)=\sum_{r=1}^{R}\left(y_{r} \cdot \log \left(p_{r}\right)+\left(1-y_{r}\right) \cdot \log \left(1-p_{r}\right)\right), \tag{17} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where yr ∈ {0, 1} indicates whether the response on trial r was correct (1) or incorrect (0), R is the total number of trials used to fit the WPPM. The model-predicted accuracy pr for each trial is given by:
(18)
pr=Pr[dM2(z0,z0′)<min(dM2(z0,z1),dM2(z0′,z1))∣W]. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p_{r}=\mathbf{P r}\left[d_{M}^{2}\left(z_{0}, z_{0}^{\prime}\right)<\min \left(d_{M}^{2}\left(z_{0}, z_{1}\right), d_{M}^{2}\left(z_{0}^{\prime}, z_{1}\right)\right) \mid \mathbf{W}\right] . \tag{18} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

We selected 1,000 angles θ that span the isoluminant plane uniformly in the DKL representation. For each angle, we defined a chromatic direction vector in DKL space as dDKL = [cos(θ), sin(θ), 0]⊤, where the first two elements correspond to the L–M and S axes of the DKL space, and the third element is set to zero to constrain the direction to the isoluminant plane (i.e., no change in luminance). For each direction, we then determined the farthest point along that vector that remained within the monitor’s gamut in linear RGB space. Specifically, we first converted dDKL to LMS cone excitations, denoted eLMS, as follows:
(S1)
eLMS=Mcone contrast→LMS⋅MΔLMS→cone contrast⋅MDKL→ΔLMS⋅dDKL⋅ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathbf{e}_{\mathrm{LMS}}=M_{\text {cone contrast } \rightarrow \mathrm{LMS}} \cdot M_{\Delta \mathrm{LMS} \rightarrow \text { cone contrast }} \cdot M_{\mathrm{DKL} \rightarrow \Delta \mathrm{LMS}} \cdot \mathbf{d}_{\mathrm{DKL}} \cdot \tag{S1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Notably, because the actual LMS cone excitations include contributions from ambient light, denoted as eLMS, ambient, we subtracted it to isolate the portion due to the RGB stimulus, denoted as eLMS, stimulus, that is,
(S2)
eLMS,stimulus=eLMS−eLMS,ambient. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathbf{e}_{\mathrm{LMS}, \text { stimulus }}=\mathbf{e}_{\mathrm{LMS}}-\mathbf{e}_{\mathrm{LMS}, \text { ambient }} \text {. }$$\end{document}

Next, we converted this isolated stimulus response into RGB space:
(S3)
dRGB=MLMS→RGB⋅eLMS,stimulus−MLMS→RGB⋅eLMS,background \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathbf{d}_{\mathrm{RGB}}=M_{\mathrm{LMS} \rightarrow \mathrm{RGB}} \cdot \mathbf{e}_{\mathrm{LMS}, \text { stimulus }}-M_{\mathrm{LMS} \rightarrow \mathrm{RGB}} \cdot \mathbf{e}_{\mathrm{LMS}, \text { background }}$$\end{document}
(S4)
=MLMS→RGB⋅eLMS,stimulus−[0.5,0.5,0.5]⊤. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle =M_{\mathrm{LMS} \rightarrow \mathrm{RGB}} \cdot \mathbf{e}_{\mathrm{LMS}, \text { stimulus }}-[0.5,0.5,0.5]^{\top} .$$\end{document}

Let Σ be the covariance matrix. The squared Mahalanobis distance between two points x0 and x1 is defined as:
(S8)
d2(x0,x1)=(x0−x1)⊤Σ−1(x0−x1) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} d^{2}\left(\mathbf{x}_{0}, \mathbf{x}_{1}\right)=\left(\mathbf{x}_{0}-\mathbf{x}_{1}\right)^{\top} \Sigma^{-1}\left(\mathbf{x}_{0}-\mathbf{x}_{1}\right) \tag{S8} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Now consider a linear transformation x0′=Ax0,x1′=Ax1\begin{document}$\mathbf{x}_{0}^{\prime}=A \mathbf{x}_{0}, \mathbf{x}_{1}^{\prime}=A \mathbf{x}_{1}$\end{document}. The corresponding transformation of the covariance matrix is Σ′ = AΣA⊤. Then the squared Mahalanobis distance in the transformed space becomes:
(S9)
d2(x0′,x1′)=d2(Ax0,Ax1)=(Ax0−Ax1)⊤⋅(AΣA⊤)−1⋅(Ax0−Ax1)=(x0−x1)⊤⋅A⊤⋅(AΣA⊤)−1⋅A⋅(x0−x1)=(x0−x1)⊤⋅A⊤⋅(A⊤)−1⋅Σ−1⋅A−1⋅A⋅(x0−x1)=(x0−x1)⊤⋅Σ−1⋅(x0−x1)=d2(x0,x1) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} d^{2}\left(\mathbf{x}_{0}^{\prime}, \mathbf{x}_{1}^{\prime}\right) & =d^{2}\left(A \mathbf{x}_{0}, A \mathbf{x}_{1}\right) \\ & =\left(A \mathbf{x}_{0}-A \mathbf{x}_{1}\right)^{\top} \cdot\left(A \Sigma A^{\top}\right)^{-1} \cdot\left(A \mathbf{x}_{0}-A \mathbf{x}_{1}\right) \\ & =\left(\mathbf{x}_{0}-\mathbf{x}_{1}\right)^{\top} \cdot A^{\top} \cdot\left(A \Sigma A^{\top}\right)^{-1} \cdot A \cdot\left(\mathbf{x}_{0}-\mathbf{x}_{1}\right) \tag{S9}\\ & =\left(\mathbf{x}_{0}-\mathbf{x}_{1}\right)^{\top} \cdot A^{\top} \cdot\left(A^{\top}\right)^{-1} \cdot \Sigma^{-1} \cdot A^{-1} \cdot A \cdot\left(\mathbf{x}_{0}-\mathbf{x}_{1}\right) \\ & =\left(\mathbf{x}_{0}-\mathbf{x}_{1}\right)^{\top} \cdot \Sigma^{-1} \cdot\left(\mathbf{x}_{0}-\mathbf{x}_{1}\right) \\ & =d^{2}\left(\mathbf{x}_{0}, \mathbf{x}_{1}\right) \end{align*}$$\end{document}

To introduce some variability, we added bivariate Gaussian noise to each comparison stimulus at the iso-distance contour in the model space. The noise standard deviation was proportional to the Euclidean distance between the reference stimulus x0 and the comparison stimulus x1. The jittered comparison stimulus x1′\begin{document}$\mathbf{x}_{1}^{\prime}$\end{document} was computed as:
(S10)
x1′=x1+ϵ,ϵ∼N(0,0.3⋅|x1−x0|2⋅I). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathbf{x}_{1}^{\prime}=\mathbf{x}_{1}+\epsilon, \quad \epsilon \sim \mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{0}, 0.3 \cdot\left|\mathbf{x}_{1}-\mathbf{x}_{0}\right|^{2} \cdot \mathbf{I}\right) . \tag{S10} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

We modeled performance using a Weibull psychometric function, which took the ΔE between the reference and jittered comparison stimuli as input and returned the predicted percent correct:
(S11)
Ψ(ΔE)=γ+(1−γ)(1−e−(ΔE/α)β)=13+23(1−e−(ΔE/3.189)1.505). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \Psi(\Delta E)=\gamma+(1-\gamma)\left(1-e^{-(\Delta E / \alpha)^{\beta}}\right)=\frac{1}{3}+\frac{2}{3}\left(1-e^{-(\Delta E / 3.189)^{1.505}}\right) . \tag{S11} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

The values of α and β were selected such that the psychometric curve returns 66.7% correct when ΔE = 2.5 (Figure S15C). A binary (correct or incorrect) response was sampled from a Bernoulli distribution using this predicted probability:
(S12)
r∼Bernoulli(Ψ(ΔE)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle r \sim \operatorname{Bernoulli}(\Psi(\Delta E))$$\end{document}

To evaluate whether the WPPM readouts systematically deviated from the ground truth, we sampled thresholds over a fine grid of reference locations (15 × 15 points evenly spaced between –0.7 and 0.7 in model space) and compared them with the corresponding ground-truth thresholds. As a comparison metric, we used the Bures-Wasserstein (BW) distance (Bhatia et al., 2019), which quantifies the dissimilarity between two positive semi-definite covariance matrices, Σ1 and Σ2. Intuitively, it captures the “effort” required to morph one ellipse into another. Mathematically, the BW distance is defined as
(S13)
d(Σ1,Σ2)=[tr(Σ1)+tr(Σ2)−2⋅tr(Σ11/2⋅Σ2⋅Σ11/2)1/2]1/2. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} d\left(\Sigma_{1}, \Sigma_{2}\right)=\left[\operatorname{tr}\left(\Sigma_{1}\right)+\operatorname{tr}\left(\Sigma_{2}\right)-2 \cdot \operatorname{tr}\left(\Sigma_{1}^{1 / 2} \cdot \Sigma_{2} \cdot \Sigma_{1}^{1 / 2}\right)^{1 / 2}\right]^{1 / 2} . \tag{S13} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

app.p:
Now define:
(S17)
Ivi(u)=∫−∞u1hK(v−vih)dv \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} I_{v_{i}}(u)=\int_{-\infty}^{u} \frac{1}{h} K\left(\frac{v-v_{i}}{h}\right) \mathrm{d} v \tag{S17} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
which is a smooth sigmoid function centered at vi, and consider the following estimate of the cumulative distribution function:
(S18)
g^(u)=1n∑i=1nIvi(u) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \hat{g}(u)=\frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} I_{v_{i}}(u) \tag{S18} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Notice that in the limit of h → 0, we recover the empirical cumulative distribution estimator ĝ = ĝemp because, in this limit, we have that Ivi(u)=1[vi≤u]\begin{document}$I_{v_{i}}(u)=\mathbf{1}\left[v_{i} \leq u\right]$\end{document}. We can further justify Equation S18 as a reasonable estimator of g by recognizing it as the integral of the density estimate in Equation S16. That is,
(S19)
g(u)=∫−∞uf(v)dv≈∫−∞uf^(v)dv=∫−∞u1nh∑i=1nK(v−vih)dv \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} g(u)=\int_{-\infty}^{u} f(v) \mathrm{d} v \approx \int_{-\infty}^{u} \hat{f}(v) \mathrm{d} v=\int_{-\infty}^{u} \frac{1}{n h} \sum_{i=1}^{n} K\left(\frac{v-v_{i}}{h}\right) \mathrm{d} v \tag{S19} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(S20)
=1n∑i=1n∫−∞u1hK(v−vih)dv=1n∑i=1nIvi(u)=g^(u) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =\frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \int_{-\infty}^{u} \frac{1}{h} K\left(\frac{v-v_{i}}{h}\right) \mathrm{d} v=\frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} I_{v_{i}}(u)=\hat{g}(u) \tag{S20} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Our refined estimator ĝ is clearly differentiable whenever we choose K(v) to be a smoothly differentiable function. In our model fitting routine, we chose K(v) to be the density of a standard logistic distribution:
(S21)
K(v)=exp⁡[−v](1+exp⁡[−v])2 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} K(v)=\frac{\exp [-v]}{(1+\exp [-v])^{2}} \tag{S21} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
